---

<ModuleLayout mod={mod}>

  

    <div class="module-header">
      <i class="fa-solid fa-film module-header-icon-accent"></i>
      <div>
        <div class="cinema-text-2">Module 34 — Deep Dive</div>
        <h1 class="module-title module-heading">Cinema</h1>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="module-subtitle">The youngest art — moving images, time, and the grammar of film</p>
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         PART A · WHAT CINEMA IS
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-a-what-cinema-is-the-foundations">Part A · what cinema is — the foundations</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">The synthesis art</p>
      <p class="cval">An art form that contains all others — and adds something new</p>
      <p class="cnote">Cinema is the youngest of the major art forms — invented in 1895 by the Lumière brothers — and the most synthetic: it combines image (painting, photography), movement, time, narrative (literature), sound (music), and performance. Nothing else can do all of these simultaneously. But cinema is not simply the sum of its ingredients: the cut — the instantaneous jump from one image to another — is a technique unique to film, with no equivalent in any other art. Eisenstein called it the primary unit of cinematic meaning: not the shot itself but the collision between shots. A film is built from photographs (24 per second) but it is not photography; from stories but it is not literature; from music but it is not music. It is something new: images in time, experienced collectively in darkness.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="cinema-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="clabel">Control of attention</p>
        <p class="cval">The camera tells you what to see</p>
        <p class="cnote">Unlike theatre or painting, cinema absolutely controls what the viewer sees and when. The close-up of a hand reaching for a gun, the cutaway to a reaction, the decision to hold on a face for five seconds or half a second — all of these are choices that determine meaning. The director and editor decide what exists in the viewer's consciousness at every moment. This totalitarian control of attention is cinema's most fundamental property.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="clabel">Controlled time</p>
        <p class="cval">The only art form that controls its own duration</p>
        <p class="cnote">A novel is read at the reader's pace; a painting can be looked at for seconds or hours. Cinema runs at a fixed rate and the viewer has no control over duration. Slow motion extends time; fast motion compresses it; the cut eliminates it entirely. Cinema can show you ten years in ten seconds (a montage) or ten seconds in ten minutes (Tarkovsky). No other art form manipulates the experience of time so directly.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="clabel">Collective experience</p>
        <p class="cval">Darkness, a crowd, and a projected light</p>
        <p class="cnote">Cinema was designed for collective experience in a darkened room — unlike literature (private) or painting (variable). The theatre of the cinema screening, with its shared reactions, its communal darkness, and the sheer scale of projection, is part of the art form. Watching a film alone on a laptop is a different experience from watching it in a cinema — not just in quality but in kind.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="clabel">Indexical reality</p>
        <p class="cval">Light actually reflected from the real world</p>
        <p class="cnote">A film is made of photographs: light physically reflected from the world exposed onto a light-sensitive surface (or its digital equivalent). This gives cinema a different relationship to reality than painting or drawing — a photograph of a face is caused by that face. Bazin argued that cinema has an "ontological realism": even fiction films contain an irreducible residue of the real. CGI complicates this but does not eliminate it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Shot types explorer -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="panel-title">Shot types — the basic vocabulary of film language</div>
      <div class="shot-grid" id="shotGrid"></div>
    </div>
    <div id="shotDetail" class="shot-detail"></div>

    <!-- Aspect ratio visualiser -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="panel-title">Aspect ratios — the shape of the frame</div>
      <div class="button-row-spaced" id="arButtons"></div>
      <div class="cinema-row">
        <div id="arFrameWrap" class="ar-wrap arframewrap">
          <div id="arFrame" class="ar-frame"></div>
          <div class="muted-text-small" id="arLabel"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ardesc" id="arDesc"></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Cinema timeline -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="panel-title">Cinema history — major periods and movements</div>
      <div class="pc-axis">
        <span class="pc-axis-mark pc-axis-mark-style">1895</span>
        <span class="pc-axis-mark pc-axis-mark-style-2">1927</span>
        <span class="pc-axis-mark pc-axis-mark-style-3">1950</span>
        <span class="pc-axis-mark pc-axis-mark-style-4">1970</span>
        <span class="pc-axis-mark pc-axis-mark-style-5">1990</span>
        <span class="pc-axis-mark pc-axis-mark-style-6">Now</span>
      </div>
      <div class="pc-row">
        <div class="pc-label">Silent era</div>
        <div class="pc-track"><div class="pc-fill pc-fill-style"><span class="pc-fill-label text-stone-800">Méliès, Griffith, Chaplin, Keaton</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pc-row">
        <div class="pc-label">Studio system</div>
        <div class="pc-track"><div class="pc-fill pc-fill-style-2"><span class="pc-fill-label text-amber-900">Hollywood Golden Age</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pc-row">
        <div class="pc-label">Italian Neorealism</div>
        <div class="pc-track"><div class="pc-fill pc-fill-style-3"><span class="pc-fill-label text-red-900">De Sica, Rossellini</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pc-row">
        <div class="pc-label">French New Wave</div>
        <div class="pc-track"><div class="pc-fill pc-fill-style-4"><span class="pc-fill-label text-blue-900">Godard, Truffaut, Chabrol</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pc-row">
        <div class="pc-label">New Hollywood</div>
        <div class="pc-track"><div class="pc-fill pc-fill-style-5"><span class="pc-fill-label text-green-800">Coppola, Scorsese, Kubrick</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pc-row">
        <div class="pc-label">Contemporary / Digital</div>
        <div class="pc-track"><div class="pc-fill pc-fill-style-6"><span class="pc-fill-label text-indigo-900">Global auteurs + streaming</span></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Director style comparison scale -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="inline-card-title">Filmmaking approaches — where major directors sit</div>
      <div class="cinema-text-3">Approximate positions on key cinematic dimensions. Tendencies, not definitions.</div>

      <div class="cinema-text-4">Style: Classical — Experimental / Formal</div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Spielberg</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Kubrick</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-2"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Godard</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-3"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Tarkovsky</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-4"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Hitchcock</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-5"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-poles">
        <span>Genre entertainment, invisible technique</span>
        <span>Self-conscious form, art cinema</span>
      </div>

      <div class="cinema-text-5">Pace: Fast-cut / Dynamic — Long takes / Contemplative</div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Michael Bay</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-6"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Scorsese</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-7"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Wong Kar-wai</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-8"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Tarkovsky</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-9"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-poles">
        <span>High-energy editing, constant motion</span>
        <span>Stillness, duration as meaning</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         PART B · FILM LANGUAGE
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-b-film-language-the-grammar-of-cinema">Part B · film language — the grammar of cinema</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">Why "grammar" is the right word</p>
      <p class="cval">Film has rules — but they can be broken deliberately</p>
      <p class="cnote">Every film in the dominant Hollywood tradition is made according to a set of conventions so thoroughly internalised that audiences never notice them: the 180-degree rule keeps characters on consistent sides of the frame across cuts; eyeline matching makes two separate shots feel like two people looking at each other; the establishing-shot to medium-shot to close-up hierarchy orients the viewer in space. These rules are the grammar of invisible editing, articulated by filmmakers like D.W. Griffith between 1910 and 1920 and refined throughout the studio era. The rules matter because understanding them is the only way to understand why breaking them — as Godard, Kubrick, or Tarkovsky do — creates such a specific effect. A jump cut is meaningless unless you know it violates the grammar.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Editing types explorer -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="panel-title">Editing — the art of the cut</div>
      <div class="button-row-compact" id="editTabs"></div>
      <div class="editpanel" id="editPanel"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Camera movement elem grid -->
    <div class="cinema-label-2">Camera movement — seven ways to move through space</div>
    <div class="elem-grid" id="camGrid"></div>
    <div id="camDetail" class="elem-detail elem-detail-panel"></div>

    <!-- Mise-en-scène: lighting schematic SVG -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="mise-en-scene-everything-in-the-frame">mise-en-scène — everything in the frame</h2>
    <div class="scroll-panel">
      <div class="panel-title">Three-point lighting — the standard setup</div>
      <svg class="cinema-media" viewBox="0 0 600 280" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <!-- Background -->
        <rect width="600" height="280" fill="var(--color-background-secondary)" rx="8"/>
        <!-- Subject -->
        <circle cx="300" cy="145" r="22" fill="var(--color-text-tertiary)" opacity="0.4"/>
        <rect x="286" y="167" width="28" height="36" rx="4" fill="var(--color-text-tertiary)" opacity="0.4"/>
        <text x="300" y="225" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-text-secondary)">Subject</text>
        <!-- Key light -->
        <circle cx="160" cy="70" r="16" fill="var(--color-background-warning)" stroke="var(--color-border-warning)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="160" y="56" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-warning)" font-weight="600">KEY</text>
        <!-- Key light beam -->
        <line x1="173" y1="83" x2="283" y2="138" stroke="var(--color-text-warning)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5,3" opacity="0.6"/>
        <text x="200" y="116" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-warning)" opacity="0.8">Main source</text>
        <text x="190" y="127" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-warning)" opacity="0.8">defines shape</text>
        <!-- Fill light -->
        <circle cx="430" cy="85" r="14" fill="var(--color-background-info)" stroke="var(--color-border-info)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="430" y="71" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-info)" font-weight="600">FILL</text>
        <!-- Fill beam -->
        <line x1="418" y1="97" x2="318" y2="138" stroke="var(--color-text-info)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5,3" opacity="0.6"/>
        <text x="368" y="110" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-info)" opacity="0.8">Softens shadows</text>
        <!-- Back light -->
        <circle cx="300" cy="38" r="13" fill="var(--color-background-success)" stroke="var(--color-border-success)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="300" y="25" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-success)" font-weight="600">BACK</text>
        <!-- Back beam -->
        <line x1="300" y1="51" x2="300" y2="123" stroke="var(--color-text-success)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="5,3" opacity="0.6"/>
        <text x="330" y="82" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-success)" opacity="0.8">Separates subject</text>
        <text x="330" y="93" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-success)" opacity="0.8">from background</text>
        <!-- Labels at bottom -->
        <rect x="50" y="248" width="130" height="22" rx="4" fill="var(--color-background-warning)" opacity="0.3"/>
        <text x="115" y="262" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-warning)">Key: hard light, 45° above/left</text>
        <rect x="200" y="248" width="110" height="22" rx="4" fill="var(--color-background-info)" opacity="0.3"/>
        <text x="255" y="262" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-info)">Fill: soft, opposite key</text>
        <rect x="330" y="248" width="180" height="22" rx="4" fill="var(--color-background-success)" opacity="0.3"/>
        <text x="420" y="262" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="var(--color-text-success)">Back: behind subject, rim light effect</text>
      </svg>
      <p class="cinema-text-6">Three-point lighting was standardised in Hollywood's Golden Age (1930s-1950s). The key light is typically positioned at 45° above and to one side, the fill light reduces contrast on the shadowed side, and the back/rim light prevents the subject merging with the background. Adjusting the ratio between key and fill changes the mood: a 2:1 ratio reads as natural and warm; 8:1 reads as noir and sinister.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    <!-- Sound design range slider -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="inline-card-title">Sound design — from diegetic to non-diegetic</div>
      <div class="cinema-text-7">Move the slider to explore how sound relates to the world of the film.</div>
      <input class="u-w-100pct" type="range" id="soundSlider" min="1" max="5" value="1" oninput="updateSoundSlider(this.value)">
      <div class="slider-labels">
        <span>Fully diegetic</span>
        <span>Non-diegetic</span>
      </div>
      <div class="slider-out" id="soundOut"></div>
    </div>

         PART C · HISTORY & MOVEMENTS
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-c-history-and-movements-how-cinema-evolved">Part C · history &amp; movements — how cinema evolved</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">Why movements matter</p>
      <p class="cval">Each movement solved a problem the previous one could not</p>
      <p class="cnote">Cinema's history is not a simple progression from primitive to sophisticated. Each major movement arose in response to specific conditions — technological, political, economic, aesthetic — and posed distinct challenges to the prevailing orthodoxy. Italian Neorealism emerged from wartime rubble and the bankruptcy of Fascist spectacle cinema; it responded with non-actors, location shooting, and stories about ordinary poverty. The French New Wave emerged from a generation who had consumed thousands of films at the Cinémathèque Française; they responded to Hollywood classicism with jump cuts, direct address, and a new relationship between filmmaker and camera. New Hollywood emerged from the collapse of the studio system and the arrival of a film-school generation who had studied the Europeans. Understanding the conditions that produced each movement gives you the vocabulary to read the films.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Movement explorer panel -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="panel-title">Major movements — click to explore</div>
      <div class="button-row-compact" id="movTabs"></div>
      <div class="movpanel" id="movPanel"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    <!-- Box office top films bar chart -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="cinema-label-3">All-time highest-grossing films (adjusted for inflation, worldwide)</div>
      <div class="cinema-text-10">Inflation-adjusted to 2024 USD. Figures are estimates — historical conversion is inexact.</div>
      <div class="bo-chart-wrap" id="boxOfficeChart"></div>
    </div>

         PART D · GENRE
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->


    <h2 class="sec" id="part-d-genre-the-language-of-expectation">Part D · genre — the language of expectation</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">Genre as contract</p>
      <p class="cval">Every genre is a promise to the audience — and a set of tools for the filmmaker</p>
      <p class="cnote">Genre is not merely a marketing category — it is a set of conventions that the audience recognises and expects, which the filmmaker can then fulfil, subvert, or comment upon. A Western is not simply a film set in the American West; it is a film that engages with a specific iconography (hats, guns, frontier towns, the open landscape), a set of narrative patterns (the lone hero, the community in danger, the final confrontation), and a set of moral coordinates (civilization vs wilderness, law vs violence). When Leone made Once Upon a Time in the West (1968), he made a film saturated in Western conventions specifically in order to expose and interrogate them. Genre knowledge is the precondition for genre subversion.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Genre multi-card explorer -->
    <div class="cinema-label-2">The major film genres — click to explore conventions</div>
    <div class="elem-grid" id="genreGrid"></div>
    <div id="genreDetail" class="elem-detail elem-detail-panel"></div>

    <!-- Genre market share distribution bar -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="panel-title-spaced">Global box office by genre — approximate shares (2015–2024 average)</div>
      <div class="dist-bar" id="genreDistBar"></div>
      <div class="dist-legend" id="genreDistLegend"></div>
      <div class="cinema-text-9">Source: based on MPAA annual reports and Box Office Mojo data. Many films straddle multiple genres; primary classification used.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
         PART E · THE AUTEURS
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-e-the-auteurs-directors-as-artists">Part E · the auteurs — directors as artists</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">What makes a director an auteur</p>
      <p class="cval">Consistency of vision across multiple films — including the bad ones</p>
      <p class="cnote">Auteur theory, as developed by the Cahiers du Cinéma critics in the 1950s (Truffaut, Godard, Rivette, Chabrol, Rohmer), proposed that a director's body of work should be read as a coherent artistic statement, the way you read a novelist's novels. The test is not whether each film is a masterpiece — it is whether the same preoccupations, visual strategies, and moral universe appear across the entire body of work. Bergman's interrogation of faith and silence; Kubrick's exploration of institutional violence and human inadequacy; Tarkovsky's meditation on time, memory, and spiritual longing: these are not accidental but structural. Even their less-successful films reveal the same fingerprints. An auteur is a director whose personality is so strong it leaves traces through the machinery of commercial filmmaking.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Director profiles -->
    <div class="panel-title-spaced">Essential auteurs — click for profile and key films</div>
    <div class="dir-grid" id="auteurGrid"></div>
    <div id="auteurDetail" class="dir-detail"></div>

    <!-- Director style comparison scale (narrative vs visual) -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="inline-card-title">Auteur orientation — narrative vs visual primacy</div>
      <div class="cinema-text-3">Where each director places their primary emphasis. Neither pole is superior.</div>
      <div class="cinema-text-4">Story/character-first — Image/form-first</div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Sidney Lumet</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-10"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Billy Wilder</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-11"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Scorsese</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-12"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Wong Kar-wai</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-13"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Malick</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-14"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-row"><div class="cscale-name">Tarkovsky</div><div class="cscale-track"><div class="cscale-dot cscale-dot-style-15"></div></div></div>
      <div class="cscale-poles">
        <span>Dialogue, performance, plot mechanics</span>
        <span>Texture, duration, visual poetry</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    <!-- Global cinema timeline (auteur era) -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="panel-title">Key auteur careers — active periods</div>
      <div id="auteurTimeline"></div>
      <div class="cinema-text-8">Approximate active periods as directors, based on first feature to final film or present day.</div>
    </div>

         PART F · WORLD CINEMA
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->


    <h2 class="sec" id="part-f-world-cinema-beyond-hollywood">Part F · world cinema — beyond Hollywood</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">Hollywood's share vs the world</p>
      <p class="cval">Hollywood dominates global distribution — but not global production</p>
      <p class="cnote">Hollywood (the major American studios) accounts for roughly 70% of global box office revenue despite producing a small fraction of total films. India produces more films annually than any other country — Bollywood alone releases over 1,800 films per year, compared to Hollywood's roughly 700. China's film market overtook North America in total revenue in 2020. South Korea, Iran, Romania, Taiwan, and Argentina have each produced major internationally recognised cinematic traditions that have zero representation in mainstream Western film discourse. The consequence is a massive asymmetry in visibility: English-language critics and audiences know far more about American cinema than any other, which distorts any account of what cinema has actually done and is doing.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- World cinema tradition explorer -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="panel-title">National traditions — what each brings to cinema</div>
      <div class="button-row-compact" id="worldTabs"></div>
      <div class="movpanel" id="worldPanel"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    <!-- Global market share distribution bar -->
    <div class="content-panel">
      <div class="panel-title-spaced">Global box office by territory — approximate 2023 shares</div>
      <div class="dist-bar" id="worldDistBar"></div>
      <div class="dist-legend" id="worldDistLegend"></div>
      <div class="cinema-text-9">Includes domestic productions. North America and China together represent over half of global theatrical revenue.</div>
    </div>

         PART G · HOW TO WATCH
    ══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->


    <h2 class="sec" id="part-g-how-to-watch-active-film-viewing">Part G · how to watch — active film viewing</h2>

    <div class="card u-mb-lg">
      <p class="clabel">Watching as a skill</p>
      <p class="cval">Most people watch films but do not see them</p>
      <p class="cnote">The difference between casual viewing and active watching is the same as the difference between hearing and listening. Casual viewing follows the story — you absorb plot and character without noticing the choices that produce your response. Active watching means attending to how the film makes you feel what it makes you feel: which cut accelerates your pulse, which close-up produces identification, which piece of music anchors an emotion to a character. This requires no special training — only the habit of asking "how did they do that?" at every moment you feel something. After one or two films watched this way, the grammar of cinema becomes visible, and you begin to understand not just what films mean but how they mean.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Viewing attention multi-card grid -->
    <div class="panel-title-spaced">What to attend to — seven layers of a film</div>
    <div class="elem-grid" id="watchGrid"></div>
    <div id="watchDetail" class="elem-detail elem-detail-panel"></div>

    <!-- Watchlist difficulty calculator -->
    <div class="calc-wrap">
      <div class="inline-card-title">Film difficulty estimator</div>
      <div class="cinema-text-7">How demanding is a film likely to be? Enter rough ratings for three properties.</div>
      <div class="calc-row">
        <div class="calc-field">
          <label>Editing pace (1=very fast, 10=very slow)</label>
          <input type="number" id="calcPace" min="1" max="10" value="5">
        </div>
        <div class="calc-field">
          <label>Narrative linearity (1=fragmented, 10=very linear)</label>
