    /* ════════════════════════════════════
       AUTEUR DIRECTOR PROFILES (Part E)
    ════════════════════════════════════ */
    const auteurs = [
      { name:'Ingmar Bergman', years:'1946-2003', tag:'Swedish',
        desc:'The central preoccupation of all Bergman is the silence of God — or rather, the impossibility of knowing whether there is silence or no God at all. The Seventh Seal (1957), Through a Glass Darkly (1961), Persona (1966), Cries and Whispers (1972): every film is a sustained interrogation of faith, death, and the capacity for human love in the face of meaninglessness. His technical signature is the extreme close-up of the human face — held longer than comfortable, demanding the viewer enter a character\'s interiority. He made 57 films and television productions. Persona remains the most formally radical film in his body of work: it appears to be about two women in a house, but it is actually about the permeable boundary of identity itself.',
        films:'Wild Strawberries (1957) · Persona (1966) · Cries and Whispers (1972) · Fanny and Alexander (1982)' },
      { name:'Stanley Kubrick', years:'1953-1999', tag:'American / British',
        desc:'Kubrick is the director most obsessed with the relationship between institutional systems and individual breakdown. His films are almost always about what happens when people are subjected to extreme conditions by organisations, ideologies, or machines: the military (Paths of Glory, Dr. Strangelove, Full Metal Jacket), the nuclear family (The Shining), the hotel (The Shining again), the evolution of human violence (2001, A Clockwork Orange). His formal signature is the extreme wide-angle lens creating geometric distortion, the Steadicam tracking shot, and a compositional symmetry that makes humans appear trapped within architectures they cannot escape.',
        films:'2001: A Space Odyssey (1968) · A Clockwork Orange (1971) · The Shining (1980) · Full Metal Jacket (1987)' },
      { name:'Alfred Hitchcock', years:'1925-1976', tag:'British / American',
        desc:'Hitchcock is cinema\'s premier technician of suspense — but his best films are about something darker: the complicity of the audience in watching. Rear Window (1954) is explicitly about the pleasure of surveillance; Vertigo (1958) is about male obsession constructing a woman to match a fantasy; Psycho (1960) kills its protagonist in the first act, violating the fundamental contract of narrative cinema. His formal vocabulary — the point-of-view shot as identification, the MacGuffin as narrative engine, the wrong man as moral position — is the most influential in Hollywood history. He made no film more disturbing than Vertigo, which is about the impossibility of loving another person rather than one\'s idea of them.',
        films:'Rear Window (1954) · Vertigo (1958) · Psycho (1960) · The Birds (1963)' },
      { name:'Jean-Luc Godard', years:'1960-2022', tag:'French / Swiss',
        desc:'No director has been more consistently provocative about what cinema can and cannot do. From Breathless (1960) to Goodbye to Language (2014 — shot in 3D in a way that makes 3D seem newly possible), Godard\'s films are always arguments: about the relationship between image and text, about cinema\'s complicity with capitalism and spectacle, about the limits of narrative. The jump cut, the direct address, the Brechtian intertitle, the voice-over that contradicts the image: these are all Godard\'s formal weapons. His political radicalism in the 1970s produced some genuinely unwatchable films. His late films (Film Socialisme, 2010; The Image Book, 2018) are extraordinary assemblages of found footage and text.',
        films:'Breathless (1960) · Contempt (1963) · Pierrot le fou (1965) · Goodbye to Language (2014)' },
      { name:'Martin Scorsese', years:'1967-present', tag:'American',
        desc:'Scorsese is the supreme filmmaker of the American male in extremis — usually Italian-American, usually Catholic, usually in a moral crisis that has something to do with violence, guilt, and redemption. Mean Streets (1973), Taxi Driver (1976), Raging Bull (1980), GoodFellas (1990), The Departed (2006): all are about men who cannot escape the codes of violence and loyalty that define them. His formal signature is the extreme expressive use of popular music as soundtrack — not as background but as ironic, intensifying counterpoint to what is happening on screen. The opening of GoodFellas, with "Rags to Riches" playing over the bloody body in the trunk, is one of the defining sequences in American cinema.',
        films:'Taxi Driver (1976) · Raging Bull (1980) · GoodFellas (1990) · The Departed (2006)' },
      { name:'Andrei Tarkovsky', years:'1962-1986', tag:'Soviet / Russian',
        desc:'Tarkovsky made only seven features, but each one operates at a level of formal and philosophical ambition that places him in a category of his own. His films are about time — specifically, about the experience of duration rather than the representation of events. His signature is the long take with the moving camera, often tracking slowly through a landscape that appears to be breathing: water, fire, rain, fog. Stalker (1979), in which three men journey toward a forbidden Zone that may or may not grant wishes, is his masterpiece. The Zone is a metaphor for cinema itself: a space in which the rules of the everyday world are suspended and something spiritual might be encountered — or might not.',
        films:'Andrei Rublev (1966) · Solaris (1972) · The Mirror (1975) · Stalker (1979)' },
      { name:'Wong Kar-wai', years:'1988-present', tag:'Hong Kong',
        desc:'Wong Kar-wai is the poet of romantic loss — specifically, the way memory transforms what was actually felt into something more intense and impossible than it could have been. In the Mood for Love (2000) and 2046 (2004) are films almost entirely constructed from what is not said, not shown, not acted upon. His formal signature is the slow-motion, desaturated tracking shot through a narrow corridor or staircase; the body in motion reduced to blur; colour used not realistically but emotionally. He does not usually use a script — he shoots, assembles, reshoot, and assembles again over years. Chungking Express (1994) was shot in 23 days as a break from the production of Ashes of Time.',
        films:'Chungking Express (1994) · Fallen Angels (1995) · In the Mood for Love (2000) · 2046 (2004)' },
      { name:'Abbas Kiarostami', years:'1970-2016', tag:'Iranian',
        desc:'Kiarostami dissolved the boundary between documentary and fiction more completely than any filmmaker before him. Close-Up (1990) restages a real criminal trial (a man who impersonated director Mohsen Makhmalbaf) using the actual participants — but it is not a documentary. Where Is the Friend\'s House? (1987) uses a non-actor child in a narrative that feels like documentary observation. Taste of Cherry (1997) — which won the Palme d\'Or — ends by showing the filmmaking apparatus itself. Kiarostami\'s formal interest in self-reflexivity was not intellectual game-playing but an ethical position: the film should be honest about what it is.',
        films:'Where Is the Friend\'s House? (1987) · Close-Up (1990) · Taste of Cherry (1997) · Certified Copy (2010)' },
    ];

