    /* ════════════════════════════════════
       WORLD CINEMA EXPLORER (Part F)
    ════════════════════════════════════ */
    const worldTraditions = {
      'Japan': { title:'Japanese Cinema',
        body:'Japan has the richest single national tradition outside Hollywood. The classical period (1950s-1960s) produced three directors of the first rank: Kurosawa, Ozu, and Mizoguchi. Kurosawa (Rashomon, Seven Samurai, Ran) worked in grand genre narratives with extraordinary formal energy. Ozu (Tokyo Story, Late Spring) invented a minimalist style so distinctive it is now called "Ozuian": low camera height (eye level of someone seated on a tatami mat), pillow shots (cutaways to objects and spaces with no narrative function), almost no camera movement. Mizoguchi specialised in stories of women destroyed by patriarchal society with an unsentimental compassion rare in world cinema. Contemporary Japanese cinema (Hirokazu Kore-eda) continues the Ozu tradition of domestic minimalism.' },
      'South Korea': { title:'South Korean Cinema',
        body:'South Korean cinema emerged from near-obscurity in the 1990s to international prominence within a decade. Three directors are primarily responsible: Park Chan-wook (Oldboy, 2003; The Handmaiden, 2016) combines baroque formal sophistication with transgressive genre narratives. Lee Chang-dong (Poetry, 2010; Burning, 2018) is a slow, realist observer of Korean social conditions with extraordinary economy of means. Bong Joon-ho (Memories of Murder, 2003; The Host, 2006; Parasite, 2019) is the most commercially successful, making films that are simultaneously genre entertainments and social critiques. Parasite — a black comedy about class — won the 2020 Oscar for Best Picture, the first non-English-language film to do so.' },
      'Iran': { title:'Iranian Cinema',
        body:'Iran has produced some of the most formally inventive cinema of the past 40 years under conditions of strict Islamic censorship, which has paradoxically generated extraordinary creativity. Abbas Kiarostami (Close-Up, Taste of Cherry) dissolved the documentary/fiction boundary. Jafar Panahi has made films while under a filmmaking ban: This Is Not a Film (2011) was shot on an iPhone and smuggled out of Iran on a USB drive in a birthday cake. Mohsen Makhmalbaf and his family (the "Makhmalbaf Film House") developed a collaborative model. The Iranian New Wave\'s key aesthetic feature is a preoccupation with children — partly because children are not subject to the same censorship restrictions as adults.' },
      'Italy': { title:'Italian Cinema',
        body:'Italy\'s contribution to cinema falls into two distinct phases. Neorealism (1944-1952) is the foundation: Rossellini, De Sica, Visconti established a vocabulary of location shooting, non-professional actors, and social realism that influenced cinema globally. Then the 1960s produced the "Cinema of the Auteurs": Fellini (8½, Amarcord), Antonioni (L\'Avventura, Blow-Up), and Pasolini (Teorema, Salò). These directors had nothing in common except formal ambition and a willingness to make films that refused to be comfortable or resolved. Contemporary Italian cinema is less internationally visible, though Paolo Sorrentino (The Great Beauty, 2013) has sustained the tradition of formally ambitious, aesthetically extravagant national filmmaking.' },
      'India': { title:'Indian Cinema',
        body:'India produces more films than any other country — approximately 1,800 per year across multiple language industries (Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam, Bengali, and many others). The dominant form is Bollywood (Hindi-language commercial cinema), which has its own distinct aesthetic: musical numbers, melodrama, romantic comedy, and family values. But the most internationally significant Indian cinema has often been in other languages. Satyajit Ray\'s Apu Trilogy (Pather Panchali, 1955) is one of the greatest works in world cinema — a Bengali-language depiction of rural poverty made for almost no money, shot over years. Contemporary Malayalam cinema (The Great Indian Kitchen, Joji) is arguably the most formally adventurous sector of current Indian filmmaking.' },
      'Latin America': { title:'Latin American Cinema',
        body:'Latin American cinema is diverse enough to resist easy generalisation, but several movements have had global impact. Brazilian Cinema Novo (Glauber Rocha, Nelson Pereira dos Santos, 1960s) fused Neorealism with a radical politics of underdevelopment and a distinctly tropical, baroque visual style. Argentine cinema has produced two directors of the first rank: Lucrecia Martel (La Ciénaga, The Headless Woman) is a formally extraordinary observer of provincial bourgeois decay; Pablo Trapero specialises in dark social realism. Alfonso Cuarón, Alejandro González Iñárritu, and Guillermo del Toro (the "Three Amigos") became internationally prominent Hollywood directors without abandoning their Mexican cultural roots.' },
      'Africa': { title:'African Cinema',
        body:'African cinema is massively underdiscussed in Western critical discourse despite producing works of major importance. The foundational figure is Ousmane Sembène (Senegal), who made Black Girl (1966) — widely considered the first sub-Saharan African feature film — and Moolaadé (2004), a devastating film about female genital mutilation. The broader African cinema tradition includes the Francophone West African films of Souleymane Cissé, Idrissa Ouedraogo, and Djibril Diop Mambéty. The vast Nigerian video film industry (Nollywood), which emerged in the 1990s with cheap VHS production, is now the world\'s third-largest film industry by volume, producing thousands of films annually for domestic African audiences on no budget.' },
    };

    function buildWorldTabs() {
